\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$; the standard number fields have their usual meanings. All additional conventions are stated locally.


\paragraph{Definitions and notation.}A mixed mock modular form combines mock-modular and modular factors under a specified convention; its nonholomorphic completion encodes the shadow. For a Fourier expansion $f(\tau)=\sum a_nq^n$, an L-series is a Dirichlet series built from its coefficients, typically $\sum_{n\ge1}a_n n^{-s}$ in a region of convergence. Growth, cusps and completion may affect analytic continuation and functional equations.

\paragraph{Catalogue wording retained for traceability.}
Do the L-series of mixed mock modular forms have interesting properties?
\paragraph{Scope and source qualification.}The source asks about interesting properties without prescribing a single theorem. No Euler product, universal functional equation or convergence condition is silently imposed. The catalogue title/status was paired with a machine-generated research attempt; that attempt is not peer-reviewed evidence of settlement. The statement covers the original source questions rather than only the title’s subcase.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Identify significant analytic or arithmetic properties of L-series attached to mixed mock modular forms.

